HST Office: a parked document tab gets its menubar back when its module returns to a toolbar mode.

Two Writer tabs, A parked: cycling the UI mode on B through ribbon modes back to Standard Toolbar left A,
once shown, with its toolbars but no menubar (1.0.1 did the same). Traced over UNO, per step, the menubar
state of both frames: entering a ribbon mode hides the menubar of every frame of the module
(SfxNotebookBar::ShowMenubarForModuleOf, from StateMethod of the frame that gets the ribbon), parked A
included. Going back, .uno:ToolbarMode runs SID_NOTEBOOKBAR per frame, whose CloseMethod closes B's ribbon and
shows only B's menubar; StateMethod then finds no ribbon to close and does nothing, and A never had a
ribbon of its own. Nothing showed A's menubar again.

SID_TOOLBAR_MODE now shows the menubars of the whole module after it applied a mode without a ribbon. In a
ribbon mode nothing changes (the ribbon setup decides, by its HasMenubar). Regression check:
tests/runtime/check-ui-mode-tabs.py and check-windows-ui-mode-tabs.py judge the menubar on every tab again.

License: modified upstream files keep their license (MPL-2.0).

--- a/sfx2/source/appl/appserv.cxx
+++ b/sfx2/source/appl/appserv.cxx
@@ -1224,6 +1224,12 @@
                 pViewFrame = SfxViewFrame::GetNext(*pViewFrame);
             }
 
+            // HST Office: a ribbon mode hid the menubar of every frame of the module (parked document
+            // tabs too, SfxNotebookBar::ShowMenubarForModuleOf); a tab without a ribbon of its own to close
+            // kept it hidden after the module went back to a toolbar mode. Show the module's menubars again.
+            if (xCurrentFrame.is() && !sfx2::SfxNotebookBar::IsActiveFor(xCurrentFrame))
+                sfx2::SfxNotebookBar::ShowMenubarForModuleOf(xCurrentFrame, true);
+
             bDone = true;
             break;
         }
